WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.302
The Kronecker–Capelli method and solving
systems of

2
00:00:04.302 --> 00:00:07.390
linear equations. In this lesson we will look
at

3
00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.319
methods for solving systems of linear
equations.

4
00:00:10.319 --> 00:00:13.920
In a higher mathematics course, systems of
linear equations

5
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.060
can be studied both as separate tasks and as
part of solving complex problems.

6
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:26.520
Systems of linear equations are used in all
branches of higher mathematics.

7
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.500
A system of linear algebraic equations is a
system of m equations in n unknowns.

8
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:41.100
The numbers aᵢⱼ are called the coefficients of
the system,

9
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:48.180
the numbers bⱼ are its constant terms, and x₁,
…, xₙ are the unknowns to be found.

10
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.490
It is convenient to write this system in
matrix form.

11
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.740
Here A is called the coefficient matrix:

12
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.410
the matrix made up of the coefficients of the
system.

13
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.159
X is the column vector of the unknowns,

14
00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:08.870
and B is the column vector of the constant
terms.

15
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.205
The augmented matrix of the system is the
system matrix

16
00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:16.990
extended by the column of constant terms.

17
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:23.560
A solution of the system is a set of n values
of the unknowns that

18
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.000
turn every equation of the system into a true
equality.

19
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.780
Any solution of the system can be written as a
column matrix.

20
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.360
A system is called consistent if it has at
least one solution, and

21
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.600
inconsistent if it has no solutions at all.

22
00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.225
To solve a system means to determine whether
it is consistent

23
00:01:50.225 --> 00:01:52.990
or inconsistent and, if it is consistent,

24
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.370
to find its general solution.

25
00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.023
Before looking for a solution of a system of
linear equations,

26
00:02:02.023 --> 00:02:04.600
we need to establish whether it is consistent.

27
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.520
The question of whether a system of linear
equations

28
00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:13.100
is consistent or inconsistent is answered by
the Kronecker–Capelli theorem.

29
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:19.810
Let us state it. For a system of m equations
in n unknowns (m and n may be equal)

30
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.264
to be consistent, it is necessary and
sufficient

31
00:02:23.264 --> 00:02:26.070
that the rank of the coefficient matrix

32
00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:32.750
equals the rank of the augmented matrix, that
is,

33
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.190
the rank of the coefficient matrix equals the
rank of the augmented one.

34
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.156
The Kronecker–Capelli theorem has the
following corollaries.

35
00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:45.700
A system of linear algebraic equations

36
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.460
is consistent only when the rank of its
augmented matrix

37
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.280
equals the rank of its coefficient matrix.

38
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:58.620
If the rank of a consistent system equals the
number of unknowns,

39
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.680
the system has a unique solution.

40
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.660
If the rank of a consistent system is less
than the number of unknowns, the system

41
00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.840
has infinitely many solutions.

42
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:19.120
Let us see in an example how the
Kronecker–Capelli theorem is used

43
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.622
to determine whether a system is consistent.

44
00:03:21.622 --> 00:03:25.490
Take a system of linear equations; we need to
study its consistency.

45
00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:31.530
If the system is consistent, we must state the
number of solutions.

46
00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:38.474
Let us form the augmented matrix. To determine
the solutions of the system,

47
00:03:38.474 --> 00:03:45.710
we need its coefficient matrix and its
augmented matrix. Let us write them down.

48
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.096
We compute the rank of the augmented matrix.

49
00:03:52.096 --> 00:03:55.360
Since matrix A is part of the augmented
matrix,

50
00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:02.070
we will find the rank of A at the same time.
For convenience,

51
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:08.320
we swap the first and second rows. We multiply
the entries of the first row

52
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.380
by minus three and add them to the
corresponding entries of the second row.

53
00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.601
Next, we multiply the entries of the first row
by

54
00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:18.560
four and add them to the entries of the third

55
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.820
row.

56
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.894
We multiply the entries of the second row by
minus two and

57
00:04:25.894 --> 00:04:28.970
add them to the corresponding entries of the
third row.

58
00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:35.760
We have brought the augmented matrix to
echelon form. The resulting echelon matrix

59
00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.581
has three nonzero rows, so its rank is three.

60
00:04:38.581 --> 00:04:41.840
By transforming the entries of the augmented
matrix,

61
00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:48.720
we simultaneously transformed the entries of
matrix A to the left of the bar,

62
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.710
so the rank of A is also three.

63
00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:59.128
By the Kronecker–Capelli theorem the system is
consistent,

64
00:04:59.128 --> 00:05:02.370
so a solution exists. To state the number of
solutions,

65
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:09.030
note that our system has three unknowns. Since
the number of unknowns

66
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.909
n is three and the rank is three,

67
00:05:11.909 --> 00:05:17.230
the system is determinate, that is, it has a
unique solution.

68
00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:25.830
To sum up. To solve a general system of linear
algebraic equations,

69
00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:32.290
we first use the Kronecker–Capelli theorem to
establish whether it is consistent.

70
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.810
If the rank of the coefficient matrix is not

71
00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.170
equal to the rank of the augmented matrix,

72
00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:43.543
we conclude that the system is inconsistent.
